  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Flood Risk Mitigation and Early Action for Vulnerable Populations in White Nile, Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">White Nile State is facing a highly probable flood crisis projected to peak between 17&#x2013;19 August 2025, according to multiple converging scientific forecasts. The Copernicus Global Flood Awareness System (GloFAS) indicates a more than 85% probability of severe flooding, based on forecasted river discharge levels at critical monitoring points, including Al Jabalain and Qitina . This is compounded by rainfall projections from NOAA,IGAD, and ZOOM Earth showing 55&#x2013;65% above-average precipitation, especially over the Ethiopian Highlands and the upper White Nile basin.
These forecasts are reinforced by a strong historical pattern: from 2020&#x2013;2023, White Nile experienced devastating floods each August, with infrastructure damage, mass displacement, and disease outbreaks (including cholera and malaria). In 2021 alone, over 94,000 people were directly affected in the state, and in 2023, more than 400 houses were destroyed in a single flood event in Al Jabalain.</narrative>
  </description>
